the decades-long opioid epidemic in the  U.S was once dominated by drugs like  heroin and oxy but with the rise of  fentanyl perhaps the most dangerous  phase is now upon us  in the first two years the pandemic more  people were killed by fentanyl than ever  before the coveted pandemic helped pave  the way for fentanyl's Ascent as  isolation and boredom drove people to  substance abuse there have been more  than 165 000 opioid deaths since the  start of covet most of those deaths  involved synthetic opioids like fentanyl  fentanyl was first developed as a  stronger kind of painkiller it's cheap  and the street version is abundant it's  more than 100 times more powerful than  morphine and 50 times more powerful than  heroin just two milligrams of fentanyl  around the size of a few grains of salt  can cause a fatal overdose fentanyl is  now infiltrating other kinds of drugs  including heroin and cocaine as well as  counterfeit prescription pills that  means people with an opioid addiction  are at risk and so are users of cocaine  black market Adderall meth marijuana and  other substances powerful drug is often  used to strengthen or stretch sock piles  of other illicit drugs the precursor  chemicals used to make fentanyl are also  globally available according to the Drug  Enforcement Administration a lot of  illicit fentanyl is made abroad and  smuggled into the U.S through Mexico in  Florida Governor Ron DeSantis has  described this situation as a problem of  law enforcement and border control he  has responded by increasing Florida's  penalties for opioid cell and  distribution and he announced a program  that connects patients with treatments  like Naltrexone and methadone to help  treat addiction similar techniques like  syringe exchanges are part of what's  known as harm reduction a movement to  decrease the risks associated with drug  use but some harm reduction techniques  have been rejected by conservative  elected officials who say they instead  enable drug use another tactic is the  distribution of fentanyl strips which  can be used to test for the powerful  substance in other drugs but the strips  are considered illegal drug  paraphernalia in about half of U.S  states including Florida according to  Kaiser health news Florida's  republican-controlled legislature  recently shut down an effort to change  that in Florida which skews older and is  more than a quarter hispanic most of  those who are dying of opioid overdoses  are white and more than half are younger  than 45 but with fentanyl use on the  upswing the death rate among black  people has overtaken white people and  overdoses among Native Americans are  also increasing disproportionately
 

